// Expected verdict from J. Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models", PhD thesis, University of Edinburgh, 2008; checked here under sMRD.
// Redundant write after read elimination: the source program, with the write
// x := r1 that the transformation removes.
//
// Source: Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008), §5.3.1.
//
// The thesis: no well-behaved execution has a read-write race, and r2 := x
// must see x := r1, which overwrites any other write, so r1 = r2 in every
// execution and r1 = 1, r2 = 2 is FORBIDDEN. Once the compiler removes
// x := r1, the JMM allows it through the write-write race between the first two
// threads, which hold different locks; that is the transformed program,
// "Redundant Write after Read Elimination (transformed).lit".
//
// The assertion used to be allow -- the transformed program's JMM verdict --
// over a copy with one anonymous lock, and passed only because MoRDor treated
// lock and unlock as no-ops.
//
x := 0;
{
  lock m1;
  x := 2;
  unlock m1;
} ||| {{
  lock m2;
  x := 1;
  unlock m2;
} ||| {
  lock m1;
    lock m2;
      r1 := x;
      x := r1;
      r2 := x;
    unlock m2;
  unlock m1;
}}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 2) [sMRD]
